Join the ESCALATION study and help shape the future of cancer screening. We are looking for an ambitious PhD student/Researcher in Training who will investigate the feasibility of early detection of multiple cancer types using blood-based circulating tumor DNA (ctDNA), combined with genetic and environmental risk assessment. You play an important role in coordinating this innovative international, multidisciplinary study and bring together clinical and molecular data to explore how early detection of cancer can be made more effective and personal.
Early detection and interception of cancer can reduce the burden of disease, mortality, loss of quality of life and high healthcare costs. Blood tests based on circulating tumour DNA (ctDNA) for early detection of multiple cancers (MCED) have shown promising results and offer a new opportunity to develop population-oriented cancer screening. In addition, improving individual risk assessment using hereditary polygenetic risk scores (PRS) and exposure risk scores (ERS) offers opportunities to further optimise the effectiveness of screening. However, there are still important challenges to the application of MCED tests within screening programmes. For example, there is no proof-of-concept for the organisation of screening on multiple cancers within a programmatic setting. In addition, the ethical, legal and social implications (ELSI) have so far been hardly investigated. In addition, cost-effectiveness plays an important role in political decision-making on the introduction of screening, including in the case of ctDNA-based screening on multiple cancers.
The ESCALATION study aims to examine all these aspects in order to evaluate the feasibility of ctDNA-based screening on multiple cancers, combined with PRS and ERS, in the Dutch context. The ESCALATION project is an international, multidisciplinary public-private partnership led by the Netherlands Cancer Institute (NKI), in close collaboration with Sanquin, Integraal Cancer Center Netherlands (IKNL), Pathological-Anatomic National Automated Archive (PALGA), the National Institute for Public Health and the Environment (RIVM), the Dutch Federation of Cancer Patient Organisations (NFC), Amsterdam UMC, Utrecht University, Johns Hopkins University (Baltimore, United States) and the company Delfi Diagnostics (Baltimore, United States).
Why the AVL?
In the Antoni of Leeuwenhoek we all work for the same goal: the best care for every patient and every kind of cancer. Not nothing. But not impossible. Here science and care work together on innovation. We find new ways that help people with cancer worldwide. Here we save lives, gain time and quality.
What are you bringing?
You have a clinical background and/or a completed master's degree in (bio) medical sciences;
You have affinity with the field of oncology;
You are proactive and have excellent communication and organisational skills;
You are excited to work within a multidisciplinary environment for translational clinical research;
You master the Dutch language very well;
Experience with clinical studies and strong affinity with biomarkers and innovative molecular diagnostics are a pré-, but not a requirement.
What are we offering you?
You are part of a dynamic international research team and collaborate with researchers with a variety of fields of expertise. During your PhD you will be given the opportunity to take high-quality courses offered by the OOA Graduate School Oncology. And further, the basis for your terms of employment is in accordance with the Cao Hospitals. We'll give you:
A four-year contract for 36 hours a week;
A gross monthly salary between €3,813,- and €4,629,- based on a 36-hour working week, appropriate to the OIO scale and depending on your experience;
144 holiday hours and 57 hours of Personal life phase budget, with full-time employment;
8.3% annual holiday allowance and a fixed end-of-year allowance of 8.33%;
A travel allowance of € 0.23 per km;
Free parking at the Antoni van Leeuwenhoek, but with our bike control and discount on the OV we also make it attractive for you to leave the car;
A great opportunity in a specialised hospital where you can also continue to learn and grow yourself if you want: The AVL Academy provides innovative and inspiring education in the field of oncological expertise, skills and personal development;
Opportunity to exercise at our Fit Boutiqs;
An active staff association and Young AVL: associations organising sporting, cultural and creative activities and events;
An employer contribution of 50% to your pension at Pension Fund Healthcare & Wellness and a collectivity discount at health insurance companies IZZ, Care and Security and Silver Cross.
