As a researcher salt marsh ecology you work on the salt marsh research of Wageningen Marine Research (WMR). Together with the salt marsh team, you will perform measurements and analyses on the long-term vegetation development and sludge, the effects of soil reduction by gas extraction and carbon storage on salt marshes. You process this knowledge in reports and give advice on salt marsh recovery plans and management. You will thus also contribute to the development of "natural-based solutions" in coastal areas and how they can be applied in an environmentally sound way.
You work together with your fellow officers on multiple projects at the same time and the responsibilities are divided based on capacity and personal interests. Contacts with customers and other stakeholders are an integral part of our work.
Your duties and responsibilities as researcher salt marsh ecology are:
annual monitoring of sludge and vegetation development on various salt marshes in the Wadden Sea;
analysis and interpretation of monitoring data;
the writing of reports to customers;
assist in the acquisition of new projects and in the writing of research proposals;
publishing and presenting own research in scientific journals and conferences.
Wageningen Marine Research
Your area of work is mainly the Dutch Wadden Sea, but other parts of the Netherlands and international projects are also possible. Wageningen Marine Research (WMR) is leading the Netherlands in the field of ecological data collection at sea: bedbugs, marine mammals, fish, birds, coral, shellfish, etc.. Many of these datasets have been collected and maintained standardized for decades. You contribute to the continuation of the salt marsh dataset with more than 60 years of data.

QUIREMENTS:
You are an entrepreneurial researcher with knowledge of the ecology of salt marshes, shavings and salty vegetation and have a wide interest in the Wadden and coastal ecosystem. You can handle time pressure and you can plan work around deadlines. You are driven, initiative-driven, focused on collaboration, customer-oriented and communicative strong.
You also have:
a completed relevant academic training in the Natural Sciences or Life Sciences and a specialisation in Wadden and coastal ecosystems. A promotion in the field described for this function is a pre-requisite;
knowledge of salt marsh vegetation;
experience with .a.Nature based solutions .a.;
a smooth pen in writing reports;
experience with statistical analyses to analyse long-term and spatial data;
driving licence B;
The Dutch language is a pré.
In our international working environment, more and more is being communicated in English. For this function, language level C1 is involved. If you do not have this level yet, we will help you of course and you can contact our language centre Wageningen in .
You see there are plenty of challenges ahead of you. These challenges can also be seen as development opportunities. So if you do not have the track record that is requested in the job vacancy, but do have solid work experience, we would also like to invite you to apply.

