Intersectional gender-sensitive view of mobility planning
Urban mobility systems are not equally accessible, affordable, comfortable or safe for everyone. For example, women and gender minorities regularly adapt their daily movements through urban space when they feel uncomfortable. This experience can also be enhanced by other forms of marginality, such as limitation, age, sexuality, ethnicity, race, class, religion, migration background and care tasks. As a result, some groups avoid certain routes, means of transport or places, from teenage girls who in some places do not feel welcome to people with disabilities who need to do extensive research into accessibility before leaving home. This limits their ability to participate fully in social life.
However, these experiences are often not sufficiently recognised in mobility planning and the design of public space. Policies are often gender-insensitive and do not take sufficient account of both social and spatial differences. This is due, among other things, to a lack of data, the dominance of quantitative transport research, limited diversity among transport researchers and planners, and research and planning priorities that marginalise specific groups and issues.
The project Genered Mobilitys, led by Anna Nikolaeva, investigates how everyday, by among others. gender-based mobility experiences are overlooked in mobility planning, and how greater gender sensitivity and intercultural awareness can be achieved.
Here's what you're gonna do.
In an earlier phase of this project, policy makers, activists and researchers were interviewed in Amsterdam, Santiago and Barcelona. As a junior researcher, you conduct research that is central to the next phase of the project: a case study on gender-related and inter-sectional mobility experiences in Amsterdam which are currently not sufficiently integrated into mobility planning. Your research is supervised by Anna Nikolaeva and Fenne Pinkster.
The mobility experiences of children with disabilities and their mothers, teenage girls, LGBTQIA+ persons, women with care tasks or other groups whose daily mobility needs are not addressed adequately include topics. The project examines how gender is related to other dimensions of identity, social position and social-spatial marginalisation, and how this affects people's ability to move around the city. However, the case may also have been a neighbourhood, a street, a project or a place viewed from the point of view of the problem that is central to this project.
The subject and methodology of the case study will be determined in consultation with your supervisors. This is a clear connection to the objectives of the project, feasibility and a good match with your skills and background.
Your work will consist of:
conducting a small-scale preparatory literature study on the selected subject and appropriate methods for the case study;
preparing and performing quality field work in Amsterdam;
analysing qualitative data;
contributing to the writing of at least one scientific article based on the research;
contributing to knowledge sharing on gender-sensitive and inter-sectional mobility planning, for example by organising events, writing blogs and contacting societal stakeholders.
We're asking you to do this.
You've got:
a master's degree, preferably in urban planning, social geography, urban studies, sociology or a related field of expertise;
experience in quality research;
a strong interest in mobility and public space, gender, interculturality, exclusion and related theme performance;
interest in connecting academic research with everyday urban experiences and public debate;
excellent analytical skills;
excellent control of Dutch; knowledge of other languages that may be relevant to possible case studies is a pre-requisite;
excellent academic writing skills in English.
