GeoLab is the central laboratory of the Faculty of Geosciences where very diverse research into the Earth system is often carried out using advanced analytical equipment . As an analytical-chemical analyst for water analysis, you will join our team by analysing the chemical composition of all kinds of water samples.
What are you gonna do?
The GeoLab is divided into several lab units with each of their own expertise. In the water lab you analyse the chemical composition of different types of water samples. These may include samples of surface water, seawater and pore water. For analysis, you use an ion chromatograph, a discrete analyzer for example the spectrophotometric determination of macronutrients and a DOC analyzer for measuring dissolved carbon compounds. You perform the analyses for our students, scientists and sometimes external clients and report the detailed measurement results. You are responsible for the proper functioning of the equipment and the quality control of the measurements.
The research is constantly evolving and requires new or adapted methods. With your knowledge of the chemistry of complex solutions (such as seawater and pore water matrices), you develop methods for the available analytical equipment that are even more suited to the needs of the researchers and preferably more sustainable. In addition, you advise the lab users (e.g. students) about sampling and pre-processing where necessary.
You mainly work in the water lab where you are supported by fellow guinea pigs from the experimental lab. In the other direction, we expect you to be able to support your fellow officers when this is necessary. The experimental lab uses the most varied experiments to carry out very varied research on geological material and environmental samples. Some examples include reactions of organic compounds under similar conditions to those on Mars and the release of phosphate from water soils under different conditions. Within the experimental lab unit we have a microbiological lab, cleanrooms and climate chambers at our disposal. Your contribution here will be mainly facilitating.
Preferably you are already familiar with company assistance (BHV) or are willing to become a company aid provider to strengthen our BHV team at the lab.
Your tasks include:
You analyse the chemical composition of water samples using the analytical equipment in the water lab and report the results you have worked out;
You are responsible for the quality control of the measurements and the proper functioning of the equipment. This includes the preparation and use of standards and reagents. Daily maintenance can be done independently;
You develop new or customized methods together with the researchers and can advise them on sampling and pre-processing where necessary;
You work closely with your fellow officers by supporting each other;
You support in fieldwork and practice and its preparation;
Preferably you are willing to act as a BHV-er in a case of calamity.

QUIREMENTS:
We are looking for a motivated and above all co-thinking and flexible analyst who likes to work in a dynamic environment with varying research questions. We also ask you to:
You have analytical-chemical HBO training or equivalent or have relevant experience, preferably in the field of water analysis. Experience with the analysis of environmental and ecological samples is a pre;
You have an independent work and thinking level and support your scientists and students by thinking along with them;
You are good at performing troubleshooting on equipment; technical insight and logical thinking are essential;
You're skilled in working with Excel;
You have preferably already gained proven experience in developing analytical methods.
You work carefully, orderly and independently but are also a good team player;
You have excellent communication skills that allow you to speak to different stakeholders (scientists, students and external customers) in both Dutch and English;
You are willing to act as a company aid provider in a case of a dangerous situation and to obtain the necessary certificates and keep them up to date.

