As part of the B-STARS (Brain STimulation to Advance Recovery after Stroke) team, you will be doing research to improve recovery after stroke using transcranial magnetic stimulation (TMS), a form of brain stimulation. TMS is a non-invasive brain stimulation treatment that can affect brain activity by means of electromagnetic induction. By targeting or inhibiting parts of brain networks, effects of regular rehabilitation treatments (physiotherapy, ergotherapy, or strategy training) can be enhanced.
Within the B-STARS team we investigate whether repetitive TMS (rTMS) can improve hand-arm function after stroke; whether we can develop personalized rTMS treatment to improve cognitive function recovery; and what the neurophysiological mechanism of action is of rTMS treatment.
As part of the team, your activities consist of:
Collection of MRI data from healthy subjects and patients with stroke;
RTMS treatment;
Maping motor and/or cognitive functions in patients after stroke by reducing function tests;
The performance of electrophysiological measurements with TMS and electromyography (EMG);
Supporting healthcare providers in the execution of research in different rehabilitation centres in the Netherlands;
Analyzing (combined) MRI, electrophysiological and functional data.
Besides learning and applying the above skills, you contribute to developing new questions and methods to answer our research questions: How can we identify patients eligible for rTMS treatment? How can we improve and personalise rTMS treatment? How can we promote the implementation of rTMS treatment in regular rehabilitation care?
You will be part of the B-STARS core team, a multidisciplinary research team of the UMC Utrecht consisting of Rick Dijkhuizen (neuroscientist), Anne Visser (revalidation doctor), Bart van der Worp (neurologist), Jord Vink (technically medical), Tessa Verhoeff (graduate student) and Sarah Tjeredsma (research assistant).
In addition, you become part of the Translational Neuroimaging (TNi) group, a research group in which we develop and apply brain imaging and technology to improve the diagnosis and treatment of cerebrovascular diseases. We do this by combining fundamental, preclinical and clinical research. The TNi group is part of the Center for Image Sciences of UMC Utrecht.
Finally, you will become part of the Knowledge Centre Rehabilitation Medicine Utrecht (KCRU), the research and innovation centre of the UMC Utrecht Brain Center and De Hoogstraat Rehabilitation. Researchers at the KCRU do scientific research on the improvement of rehabilitation treatment in collaboration with experience experts; Rehabilitators and their neighbors.
We're looking for someone with:
A relevant master's programme: (Technical) Medicine, Biomedical Technology, Neuroscience & Cognition, Clinical Neuropsychology, Biomedical Sciences or a similar course;
Interest in neurorehabilitation, stroke and neuroplasticity studies;
Affinity with non-invasive brain stimulation (TMS) and imaging techniques (MRI);
Affinity with (statistic) analysis of medical images (MRI), electrophysiology (EMG) and functional data;
Strong motivation, sense of responsibility and flexibility;
Strong social skills, which are reliable and structured. You can motivate others and be accessible to questions and help;
Good educational skills. You can transfer knowledge to fellow candidates with different backgrounds and knowledge levels. You recognize where knowledge gaps lie and act accordingly.
A salary between €3,217 and €4,077 gross per month (scale OIO), based on a full-time employment (36 hours).
An employment contract for the duration of the promotional pathway.
A place in a strong, multidisciplinary team from rehabilitation medicine, neurology/neuroscience and imaging.
Ample opportunities for personal and scientific development, including access to the Graduate School of Life Sciences of Utrecht University.
Opportunities for national and international cooperation, and participation in scientific conferences.
Annual leave of 8.3% and holiday allowance of 8%;
Favourable pension scheme at ABP: we pay 70% of the premium;
100% reimbursement of your public transport costs. You come walking, cycling or by car? Then you will receive € 0.21 per km (up to max. 40 km single journey);
Cao à la carte: Choose additional terms of employment in exchange for gross salary, such as for buying a bike, paying for your Internet subscription and memberships.

